{"id":"8db7ba79-f9a2-451d-8f22-4537a71e77d7","object":"chat.completion","created":1785027362,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case[\\\"word_token_indices\\\"]\\n    word_scores = case[\\\"word_scores\\\"]\\n    forced_words = case[\\\"forced_words\\\"]\\n    token_budget = case[\\\"token_budget\\\"]\\n    tokens_per_word = [len(indices) for indices in word_token_indices]\\n    n = len(word_token_indices)\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    selected_set = set(forced_set)\\n    used_tokens = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    used_score = sum(word_scores[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - used_tokens\\n    candidate_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\\n    capacity = remaining_budget\\n    if capacity < 0:\\n        selected_words = sorted(selected_set)\\n        retained = []\\n        for idx in selected_words:\\n            retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n        return {\\n            \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained,\\n            \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n            \\\"tokens_used\\\": used_tokens\\n        }\\n    dp = [0.0] * (capacity + 1)\\n    chosen = [set() for _ in range(capacity + 1)]\\n    for idx in candidate_indices:\\n        w = tokens_per_word[idx]\\n        v = word_scores[idx]\\n        if w < 0 or w > capacity:\\n            continue\\n        for j in range(capacity, w - 1, -1):\\n            if dp[j - w] + v > dp[j]:\\n                dp[j] = dp[j - w] + v\\n                chosen[j] = set(chosen[j - w])\\n                chosen[j].add(idx)\\n    best_val = max(dp)\\n    best_cap = dp.index(best_val)\\n    best_chosen = chosen[best_cap]\\n    all_selected = forced_set.union(best_chosen)\\n    selected_words = sorted(all_selected)\\n    retained = []\\n    for idx in selected_words:\\n        retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n    tokens_used = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in all_selected)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained,\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) for NLP-LL2-02. The task is to select words (based on scores) given a token budget, with forced words that must be included. The output should include retained_token_indices (indices of tokens kept), selected_words (indices of chosen words), and tokens_used (count of tokens used). The input case has word_token_indices (list of token index lists for each word), word_scores (list of scores for each word), forced_words (list of word indices that must be selected), token_budget (max tokens). We need to select a subset of words (including forced ones) maximizing sum of scores while total tokens <= budget. This is a knapsack like optimization. Since word_token_indices have varying lengths (tokens per word), we need to select words. Typically one would use DP (0/1 knapsack) with word weight = number of tokens, value = score. But careful: tokens are indices, and we need to output retained_token_indices (the actual token indices from selected words, presumably in order? Likely as they appear? The output schema says retained_token_indices is array of integers, selected_words array of integers (presumably indices of selected words, maybe sorted ascending?). Also tokens_used is total count.\n\nWe must implement solve(case) that returns dict with those keys. Use only Python standard library. No imports beyond possibly json but we don't need to import because we just return dict.\n\nWe need to parse case: case is the payload object. It contains \"word_token_indices\", \"word_scores\", \"forced_words\", \"token_budget\". Possibly also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n\nWe need to select words. Since forced words must be included, first include them. Then we have remaining budget. Then we need to select other words to maximize sum of scores, given remaining budget. This is typical knapsack. Since number of words may be up to? Not specified but likely manageable. Use DP over words and token capacity. But note: tokens are integer counts. We can assume total tokens per word small? Could be up to many but DP O(N * budget) fine.\n\nBut careful: forced words may include words that could also be selected as part of optimization, but we must include them regardless, so subtract their tokens from budget and add their scores. Then solve knapsack for remaining words (excluding forced ones). Also note: forced_words list might have duplicates? Assume unique.\n\nEdge: if forced words exceed budget? Then we cannot meet budget? But likely not given. We'll assume forced words always within budget.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Extract lists: word_token_indices, word_scores, forced_words, token_budget.\n- Compute tokens_per_word = [len(indices) for indices in word_token_indices].\n- Initialize selected = set(forced_words)\n- total_tokens = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in forced_words)\n- total_score = sum(word_scores[i] for i in forced_words)\n- remaining_budget = token_budget - total_tokens\n- If remaining_budget < 0: maybe need to return something? But spec says forced_words are forced, so we might have to include them even if over budget? But that would make token_used > budget? Probably test cases ensure feasibility. We'll assume feasible.\n- Get candidate words: all indices not in forced. For DP, we want to maximize score sum subject to weight <= remaining_budget. Standard 0/1 knapsack DP using list of (weight, score).\n- Use DP array size remaining_budget+1, initialized to 0, and also track chosen items. For each candidate index i (with weight w, score v), iterate budget from remaining down to w, if dp[j-w] + v > dp[j], update dp[j] and keep path. After processing all, find best score at dp[remaining_budget] or lower? Actually we want max score up to remaining_budget, so the best value is max(dp[0:remaining_budget+1])? But DP already ensures at each capacity max score. We can take dp[remaining_budget] as best for exact capacity, but we can also allow underfilling. So we need to consider all capacities <= remaining_budget. Actually DP as described for 0/1 knapsack with capacity C, dp[j] is best value for capacity j. The best overall for capacity <= C is max(dp). We can track the capacity that yields max (or simply after DP, find argmax). But we also need to know which items selected. So we can store a list of selected indices per capacity j. Use a list of sets or list of booleans. We can also store parent pointers: for each j after processing item, if we update, record that item chosen. We can reconstruct later. Since number of candidates is moderate, we can store for each j a set of indices. Simpler: after DP, we can backtrack using a 2D table? But memory fine. Use list of sets: chosen[j] = set of indices that give best value for capacity j. For each item, for j from remaining down to w, if dp[j-w] + v > dp[j], then dp[j]=dp[j-w]+v, chosen[j] = chosen[j-w] copy + {i}. If equal, we could keep one arbitrarily (but need stability? Not required). Then after processing all, find best capacity best_cap = argmax(dp[:remaining_budget+1]) (or just use remaining_budget because if we can't fill all, dp[remaining] might be same as lower capacity? Actually if we have leftover budget that cannot be used, dp[remaining] may be same as lower capacity. But we want to maximize value, so we need best value across all capacities ≤ remaining. So we can compute best_val = max(dp), best_cap = index of first occurrence? Or just get the set for that capacity. Then selected additional words = chosen[best_cap].\n- Then final selected_words sorted list of forced plus additional (ascending numeric order? Not required but typical. The output schema just says array of integers, no specific order. But to be consistent, we can sort ascending. However note: retained_token_indices should be list of token indices from selected words. The order? Likely the order they appear in the original word list? Or the order of token indices? The schema doesn't specify ordering. But example fixtures (not given outputs) might expect preserved order? Since they are token indices, it might be meaningful to keep the order of tokens as they appear in the original sequence (i.e., flatten token indices from selected words in the order of increasing word indices). That is typical: retain tokens in the order they appear. So we should sort selected word indices ascending, then for each word, append its token indices (preserving order within word). That gives retained_token_indices in original sequence order.\n- tokens_used = sum(tokens_per_word for selected words).\nPerformance: O(N * budget) where N <= maybe 50, budget maybe up to 1000.\n\nEdge: forced words may have duplicates? Not, but handle gracefully.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use Python's built-in functions.\n- Return dict with keys: \"retained_token_indices\", \"selected_words\", \"tokens_used\".\n- Ensure no mutation of case.\n- Use only standard library.\n\nWe need to output Python source as a JSON string with key \"implementation\". So we will write the function and then json.dumps it. But careful: The response must be exactly a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value is a string containing Python code. So we need to produce that JSON.\n\nWrite solve in a string, escape newlines etc. We'll construct the string carefully.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # extract\n    word_token_indices = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    word_scores = case[\"word_scores\"]\n    forced_words = case[\"forced_words\"]\n    token_budget = case[\"token_budget\"]\n    \n    tokens_per_word = [len(indices) for indices in word_token_indices]\n    n = len(word_token_indices)\n    \n    # forced words set\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\n    \n    # include forced words\n    selected_set = set(forced_set)\n    used_tokens = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in forced_set)\n    used_score = sum(word_scores[i] for i in forced_set)\n    \n    remaining_budget = token_budget - used_tokens\n    \n    # candidates indices not forced\n    candidate_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\n    \n    # DP for knapsack\n    capacity = remaining_budget\n    # dp[j] = max score achievable with exactly j tokens (or <= j) we'll treat as exactly j\n    # Use list of size capacity+1\n    if capacity < 0:\n        # cannot include forced words, but spec says feasible, just return forced? Or error? We'll still return forced, but token_used > budget? That may be invalid. But we assume not.\n        # To be safe, we still return what we have, but token_used may exceed budget.\n        selected_words = sorted(selected_set)\n        retained = []\n        for idx in selected_words:\n            retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n        return {\n            \"retained_token_indices\": retained,\n            \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n            \"tokens_used\": used_tokens\n        }\n    \n    # Initialize dp array and chosen sets\n    dp = [0.0] * (capacity + 1)\n    # We'll store for each capacity the set of chosen candidate indices (not forced)\n    chosen = [set() for _ in range(capacity + 1)]\n    \n    # Process each candidate\n    for idx in candidate_indices:\n        w = tokens_per_word[idx]\n        v = word_scores[idx]\n        if w < 0 or w > capacity:\n            continue  # can't take if weight > capacity or negative (shouldn't happen)\n        # iterate backwards\n        for j in range(capacity, w - 1, -1):\n            if dp[j - w] + v > dp[j]:\n                dp[j] = dp[j - w] + v\n                # copy set and add\n                chosen[j] = set(chosen[j - w])\n                chosen[j].add(idx)\n            # else if equal? we don't care, keep original\n    \n    # Find the best capacity giving max score (allowing underfilling)\n    best_val = max(dp)\n    best_cap = dp.index(best_val)  # first occurrence\n    best_chosen = chosen[best_cap]\n    \n    # Combine all selected words\n    all_selected = forced_set.union(best_chosen)\n    selected_words = sorted(all_selected)\n    \n    # Compute retained token indices in order\n    retained = []\n    for idx in selected_words:\n        retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n    \n    tokens_used = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in all_selected)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nPotential improvements: floating point comparison equality might cause issues, but we use strict >, so fine. Also note: dp values are floats, but comparison works.\n\nNow, wrap this in a string for JSON.\n\nWe need to produce JSON with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a string. We'll need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite the code as a multi-line string, then use json.dumps to ensure proper escaping.\n\nBut we must avoid using json module in the response (since we are not executing, just constructing). We can manually construct the JSON string. But easier: Write the code as a triple-quoted string, then replace backslashes and quotes appropriately. Alternatively, we can use the json library in our thinking to generate the string, but we need to output it as text. Since we are not in a Python environment, we can manually escape.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a string with single quotes for the outer JSON? The JSON string value must be enclosed in double quotes, and any double quotes inside must be escaped with backslash. Also escape backslashes, newlines as \\n.\n\nLet's write the code with careful indentation. We'll represent it as a single line with \\n separators? Actually JSON allows \\n within string. So we can keep newlines by using \\n. But for readability we can produce a compact one-liner? The instruction says \"encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we can include newlines as \\n, but that makes it hard to read. However the output is just a JSON object, so it's fine.\n\nWe can write the code as a string with actual newlines and then escape them when constructing the JSON. Since we are generating the JSON manually, we need to ensure each newline becomes \\n, each backslash becomes \\\\, each double quote becomes \\\".\n\nLet's write the code in a clean format:\n\nimplementation_str = '''def solve(case):\n    word_token_indices = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    word_scores = case[\"word_scores\"]\n    forced_words = case[\"forced_words\"]\n    token_budget = case[\"token_budget\"]\n    tokens_per_word = [len(indices) for indices in word_token_indices]\n    n = len(word_token_indices)\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\n    selected_set = set(forced_set)\n    used_tokens = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in forced_set)\n    used_score = sum(word_scores[i] for i in forced_set)\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - used_tokens\n    candidate_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\n    capacity = remaining_budget\n    if capacity < 0:\n        selected_words = sorted(selected_set)\n        retained = []\n        for idx in selected_words:\n            retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n        return {\n            \"retained_token_indices\": retained,\n            \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n            \"tokens_used\": used_tokens\n        }\n    dp = [0.0] * (capacity + 1)\n    chosen = [set() for _ in range(capacity + 1)]\n    for idx in candidate_indices:\n        w = tokens_per_word[idx]\n        v = word_scores[idx]\n        if w < 0 or w > capacity:\n            continue\n        for j in range(capacity, w - 1, -1):\n            if dp[j - w] + v > dp[j]:\n                dp[j] = dp[j - w] + v\n                chosen[j] = set(chosen[j - w])\n                chosen[j].add(idx)\n    best_val = max(dp)\n    best_cap = dp.index(best_val)\n    best_chosen = chosen[best_cap]\n    all_selected = forced_set.union(best_chosen)\n    selected_words = sorted(all_selected)\n    retained = []\n    for idx in selected_words:\n        retained.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\n    tokens_used = sum(tokens_per_word[i] for i in all_selected)\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }'''\n\nNow we need to escape this string for JSON. We'll replace each backslash with double backslash, each double quote with backslash-doublequote, each newline with \\n. Also tab? None.\n\nBut note: the code contains backslashes? There is none. Also contains double quotes around keys and string literals. So we need to escape those double quotes. Also we need to escape the backslash in the string? Actually there are no backslashes in the code. So we only need to escape double quotes and newlines.\n\nWe can do: implementation_escaped = implementation_str.replace('\\\\','\\\\\\\\').replace('\"','\\\\\"').replace('\\n','\\\\n')\n\nBut careful: The replacement must be done in order; we replace backslashes first (though none) then quotes, then newlines.\n\nThen the JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"escaped code\"}.\n\nWe need to output exactly that JSON object. No extra whitespace.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer as a single JSON object.\n\nLet's double-check correctness: The DP uses exact capacity; we allow underfilling by taking max over all capacities. That's correct.\n\nEdge: If remaining_budget is 0, we still process DP but candidate indices with weight 0? Not possible because tokens_per_word >=1. So dp[0]=0, best_cap=0, best_chosen empty. Works.\n\nAlso forced words may be empty, then forced_set empty, used_tokens=0, all fine.\n\nOne more nuance: The DP uses floating scores; we compare >, which is fine. But if two items have same score but different weights, we may choose one over another arbitrarily; that's acceptable.\n\nNow produce output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1187,"completion_tokens":4214,"total_tokens":5401,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3648},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":35},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}